Track 3: Aero-Zafra on the Last Gauge
- Story & Context: Long after the mine's lowest terminal was decommissioned due to thermal shifting, scavengers discovered that the three-mile ventilation pipeline had warped under tectonic compression into an acoustic miracle that whistled complex brass overtones whenever the surface barometric pressure changed. Pilgrims from the dust towns now ride the very last narrow-gauge maintenance train down into the dark every spring, bringing battered trumpets to jam alongside the mountain's natural resonant lungs. As the final departure cart rattles into the warm cavern, the entire tunnel vibrates with a self-sustaining, triumphant carnival anthem played by both living lungs and pressurized steel: "The mountain caught the fever and it never came back up!"
